WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.560
Picture this: You’ve spent hours creating
your design, only to find that, when it’s

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:09.519
printed on a t-shirt, the colors look different
from what you saw on your screen.

00:00:09.519 --> 00:00:11.759
Wondering how this happened and how to fix
it?

00:00:11.759 --> 00:00:17.109
Continue watching a learn how to color correct
for print.

00:00:17.109 --> 00:00:22.250
DTG, or direct-to-garment printing, is pretty
versatile.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:27.039
However, it’s important to note that the
colors you choose for your artwork may appear

00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:29.410
different when printed on an actual product.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:30.960
So, what causes the difference?

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.230
Why is color correcting important?

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:38.160
As an online store owner, you strive for a
great first impression, hoping to attract

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.460
returning customers.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:44.140
This requires delivering a product that matches
what customers see on their screens.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:49.190
Well, the human eye can detect about 10 million
colors, but each person sees them slightly

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:50.190
differently.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:55.970
Meanwhile, computer monitors can display over
16 million colors and each screen may show

00:00:55.970 --> 00:01:01.500
them differently, some appearing warmer with
yellow tones and others cooler with blue tones.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:08.210
That’s why your designs might look different
on various screens like computers, TVs, and

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:09.210
smartphones.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.460
Then the DTG printing process comes in.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:16.180
Several factors can affect how colors appear
on the final printed product like the type

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:21.780
of fabric, the color of the garment, whether
a white under base is used, and the color

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:25.400
spaces used in designing.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:31.070
Think of color space as a huge box of crayons,
representing all the colors you can see in

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:34.540
digital images or on screens and printers.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:39.079
Each color space has its own limits on the
colors it can display or print.

00:01:39.079 --> 00:01:43.310
So, if you choose colors outside the range
supported by a printer, it won’t be able

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:44.810
to reproduce them accurately.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:50.220
For instance, if you use a neon color that
is beyond what DTG printers can handle, the

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:53.100
final result might not look as expected.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.180
Just like how different brands of crayons
offer different color sets, your devices and

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.729
software also use different color spaces.

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:04.719
So, to get better print results, it’s important
to understand the color space you’re designing

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:05.719
in to get more accurate print results.

00:02:05.719 --> 00:02:10.690
For DTG printing, you can design in two color
spaces—RGB or CMYK.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:16.760
RGB, which stands for red, green, and blue,
is used for emitted light, like from monitors,

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.700
and offers a wider range of colors than CMYK.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:24.610
If you’re designing in RGB, make sure you
use the RGB color space in Photoshop.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:31.330
To do this, go to Edit, choose Convert to
Profile, and select RGB as the profile in

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:33.510
the Destination Space option.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:37.700
When designing in the RGB color space, you
also want to ensure that the colors you pick

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:40.420
are within the color range of DTG printers.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:45.330
You can check this by turning on the Gamut
Warning feature in Photoshop by clicking View

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:46.970
and Gamut Warning.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:51.560
With this feature, you can view and spot colors
that can’t be reproduced in RGB.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:54.280
It helps you avoid using colors that won’t
print well.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.480
Here’s how it appears in Photoshop.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:02.540
When you’re done designing in RGB, export
your design as a PNG file with the sRGB color

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:03.540
space.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:04.540
(sRGB IEC61966-2.1)

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:09.310
If you design in CMYK, which stands for cyan,
magenta, yellow, and black, you’ll already

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:13.090
be designing in colors within the color range
of DTG printers.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:18.980
However, you’ll have to switch your design
to RGB once finished and export it as a PNG

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:21.849
file with that same sRGB color space.

00:03:21.849 --> 00:03:27.430
To switch from CMYK to RGB, go to Edit in
the top menu, pick Convert to Profile, and

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:30.000
choose RGB in the Destination Space section.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.959
Select sRGB.

00:03:31.959 --> 00:03:37.500
No matter if you’re designing in RGB or
CMYK, make sure to export your final design

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:44.330
as a PNG file with an sRGB color profile for
the best-printed results.

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:50.000
To help you understand how colors appear on
screen versus in print, you can order product

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.940
samples to test your designs.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:56.270
Samples are crucial as they offer a tangible
reference for how colors will appear when

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:57.270
printed.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:00.440
Here’s a quick guide to ordering product
samples from Printful:

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:04.280
Log in to your Printful account and go to
the Printful Dashboard.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:07.080
Click New order in the top right corner.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:12.360
Choose your store (if you have more than one)
and select Create your order under Test your

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:13.360
designs.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.600
You’ll be directed to a page where you can
either select an existing design from your

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:18.840
account or create a new one.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:23.600
For this example, I’ll select one of my
existing DTG designs by clicking here.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:30.220
For optimal results in DTG printing, try testing
your designs on black and white garments.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:34.810
When you select a light-colored garment, your
design gets printed directly onto the fabric.

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:40.280
However, for darker garment colors, we add
a white underbase layer before applying your

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:41.280
design.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:44.720
This ensures that your design colors pop on
darker fabrics.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:49.330
Testing both light and dark-colored garments
will give you a full picture of how your designs

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:50.330
will look.

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:51.780
How to create custom swatches of your designs

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:56.580
Here’s a tip: If you want to test a wide
range of colors for your designs before selling

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:00.420
them, consider creating a custom color swatch
to print as a sample.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:05.860
Make a design with squares of different color
variations and include their RGB values below.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:10.460
This helps you see many colors at once and
provides a reference for future designs.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:15.810
When ordering a product sample, test not only
its main colors but also some variations to

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:17.639
see how they’ll look in the final print.

00:05:17.639 --> 00:05:20.080
I’ll do just that with this design.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:24.400
After receiving your product sample with the
custom swatch, compare the printed colors

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.210
with those on your computer monitor.

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:30.690
Decide if any adjustments are needed to match
your original vision.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:36.830
Ok, so I got my design and color swatch on
a white t-shirt and a black t-shirt with a

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:37.830
white under base.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:43.530
As you can see, each color has its RGB value,
which I can add and modify in design software

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:44.539
like Photoshop.

00:05:44.539 --> 00:05:48.951
Seeing the printed design now, I realize that
the yellow color of the plane didn’t turn

00:05:48.951 --> 00:05:54.130
out as vibrant as it appeared on the screen,
and the red colors of the ribbon lost contrast

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:57.300
because the colors ended up being too close
in tone.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:01.350
So now I’ll show you how to color correct
based on our printed swatches.

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:06.160
I’ll open the original print file, which
has the design on a transparent background.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.690
Then, I’ll create a new layer where we’ll
place our new colors.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:15.390
Next, open your color panel to add RGB values
for the new, preferred colors.

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:18.440
To do that, select Window and click Color.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:20.970
This will bring up a color panel in your workspace.

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:22.910
Right now, we’re using the Hue Cube view.

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:26.039
To change it, click here and select RGB Sliders.

00:06:26.039 --> 00:06:31.380
Here, you can enter the RGB values from the
printed color swatch variations or adjust

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:32.430
them as needed.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:36.340
We’ll use the paint bucket tool to apply
new colors to our design.

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:39.380
Keep an eye on the panel that pops up when
you select this tool.

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:42.000
Make sure the All Layers box is checked.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.700
If not, you’ll fill the entire layer with
just one color.

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:47.560
The same goes for the Contiguous box.

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:51.890
If checked, it lets you change the color within
one element only.

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:55.949
If unchecked, you’ll adjust the color of
all pixels with the same color throughout

00:06:55.949 --> 00:06:57.379
the entire design.

00:06:57.379 --> 00:07:01.879
The next steps are simple: choose a variant
color from the color swatch that better matches

00:07:01.879 --> 00:07:03.009
your design idea.

00:07:03.009 --> 00:07:08.750
Put the values in the RGB color panel, then,
with the paint bucket tool selected, click

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:10.690
on the color you want to change.

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:13.560
Once you’ve corrected your colors, save
your project file.

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:15.500
Here’s the corrected result.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:20.360
It’s closer to the original design, and
the colors are more vibrant and noticeable.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:25.729
On our screens, the colors might seem too
bright or intense, but remember, these colors

00:07:25.729 --> 00:07:28.889
are based on the RGB values from the printed
color swatch.

00:07:28.889 --> 00:07:29.889
